Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||04-09-2000||03:47 AM||||Formula, the most commonly made mistake is that people aren't feeding it with enough power. Feed it with 1.21 <B>Jiga</B>watts, not <B>Giga</B>watts... Check the manual of your Mr. Fusion.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RFMike||04-09-2000||08:20 AM||||Thanks, GTB.<BR>One thing I find interesting is the hood position on the different crashes.  The grey coupe's hood catches and stays in the safety catch at the rear of the hood on one side and releases into the windshield on the other.  The red coupe hood is into the windshield on both sides.  The hood snaps partways back from the front, or it may have gone through the windshield. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||GREENDANO||04-09-2000||09:28 AM||||I had a silver 2M4 just like the one on the sight after the test.  The front end even looked the same.  I bought that car sight unseen that the owner said was just a light front hit.  Needless the say I got my $200 worth or parts of it and scraped it soon after.<BR>  Lesson learned, don't trust what anyone says about the condition of a car....Check it out yourself.  &lt;-----DUH||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||04-09-2000||10:28 AM||||Here is a link from an NIFE club member who was T-boned by a full size pick-up truck. He was injured less than the driver of the pick-up! (pick-up driver was not wearing his seatbelt.). The 86 SE was cut in half but the guy walked away! (complete with pics) <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/art-wreck.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/art-wreck.html</A>  ||||reg||4
Z||000007||TheFiero64||04-09-2000||10:41 AM||||I wonder why it split in half like that? Good thing there was no passenger. Did you see the passenger seat all mangled on the ground? It is a safe car don't get me wrong, but WOW that thing just split in half! Most compact cars I see in side impacts look like this too. I thought the fiero would absorb the impact differently. oh well, It still is safer than most.||||reg||5
